 node2 and toward node3)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1 eth1 assigned 192.168.10.1/24 (toward node2's subnet) and eth2 assigned 192.168.20.1/24 (toward node3's subnet), both shown UP with the correct addresses, and confirmed still present/UP after node1's reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cb28dfaa410e47ee\",\"cmd-b10411b5dc5d49d1\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node1 forwards IPv4 traffic between the two VLAN interfaces\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Global and per-interface ip_forward/forwarding sysctls set to 1 on node1, and forwarding is directly confirmed functional by cross-VLAN pings showing ttl=63 (one router hop) between node2 and node3, both before and after node1's reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cb28dfaa410e47ee\",\"cmd-2bd769aea3cf41c6\",\"cmd-f3febff9ede44563\",\"cmd-b10411b5dc5d49d1\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 (each on their own VLAN/subnet) can reach each other across the VLAN boundary via node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node2 (192.168.10.2/24) and node3 (192.168.20.2/24) were each given a static route to the other's subnet via node1, and bidirectional ping succeeded end-to-end with ttl=63, confirming a routed (not direct L2) path through node1. Connectivity was reverified after all three nodes were individually restarted.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-24565d3c1bb743ef\",\"cmd-50c65f59883a42fe\",\"cmd-2bd769aea3cf41c6\",\"cmd-f3febff9ede44563\",\"cmd-48baa941f97044f1\",\"cmd-f746bb9939874ecc\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Configuration is durable/persistent (not just runtime-only ip/nmcli state)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's interface addresses and forwarding sysctls were written to /etc/network/interfaces.d/vlan-routing.conf and /etc/sysctl.d/99-antrieb.conf; node2/node3 addressing and routes were persisted via nmcli connection profiles. This was independently confirmed by a real reboot on all three nodes after which addresses, forwarding, routes, and cross-VLAN ping all recovered without manual reapplication.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-64e5dc8578c543a5\",\"cmd-fb7834152e404440\",\"cmd-f5f02fd1f61f480f\",\"cmd-b10411b5dc5d49d1\",\"cmd-d83a7682b16b4858\",\"cmd-1d72f43c095941a8\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Chronological review of state-changing executor commands shows each mutation maps to a material outcome or a necessary prerequisite: cmd-cb28dfaa410e47ee's flush of docker0/interfaces-config/networking.service/dhclient on node1 was needed to stop SONiC's interface reconciler from reverting the manual eth1/eth2 assignment, and the subsequent IP/route/sysctl commands on all three nodes directly implement the routing requirement. The three native reboots (cmd-2099c31ca14b40f5, cmd-80702735044e4a35, cmd-49dba734dd6f4008) are the independent restart-evidence protocol, executed sequentially with clear pre/post boot_id capture and recovery verification; the many failed/timeout boot_id polls immediately surrounding each reboot are expected transient connection loss during reboot, not unnecessary mutations. No troubleshooting experiments or unrelated mutations were found. Minor residue: the reboot helper left /tmp/reboot.log on node2 and node3, a small, low-risk, unremoved artifact rather than a reverted one.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cb28dfaa410e47ee\",\"cmd-2099c31ca14b40f5\",\"cmd-80702735044e4a35\",\"cmd-49dba734dd6f4008\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built a correct routed topology on node1 (two subnetted, forwarding-enabled interfaces) and demonstrated genuine cross-VLAN reachability between node2 and node3 via ttl-consistent pings, both before and after persisting the configuration through real reboots of all three nodes. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was completed for every managed node with clear boot-id transitions and full state recovery. Hygiene is good; the only blemish is an unremoved /tmp/reboot.log left by the reboot helper on node2/node3, which is bounded and low-risk.\",\"limitations\":[\"Lifecycle after_prepare is an alias of before_prepare (prepare disabled), so all observed changes are attributable to the executor by construction; this is expected given the task configuration and not evidence of any gap.\",\"Boot-id polling produced several transient connection-reset/timeout entries during each reboot window; these are expected artifacts of the node being offline mid-reboot and were correctly not treated as failures.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node1 gets a routed interface inside each VLAN (one per subnet, toward node2 and toward node3)","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1 eth1 assigned 192.168.10.1/24 (toward node2's subnet) and eth2 assigned 192.168.20.1/24 (toward node3's subnet), both shown UP with the correct addresses, and confirmed still present/UP after node1's reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cb28dfaa410e47ee","cmd-b10411b5dc5d49d1"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"node1 forwards IPv4 traffic between the two VLAN interfaces","status":"satisfied","summary":"Global and per-interface ip_forward/forwarding sysctls set to 1 on node1, and forwarding is directly confirmed functional by cross-VLAN pings showing ttl=63 (one router hop) between node2 and node3, both before and after node1's reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cb28dfaa410e47ee","cmd-2bd769aea3cf41c6","cmd-f3febff9ede44563","cmd-b10411b5dc5d49d1"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"node2 and node3 (each on their own VLAN/subnet) can reach each other across the VLAN boundary via node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 (192.168.10.2/24) and node3 (192.168.20.2/24) were each given a static route to the other's subnet via node1, and bidirectional ping succeeded end-to-end with ttl=63, confirming a routed (not direct L2) path through node1. Connectivity was reverified after all three nodes were individually restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-24565d3c1bb743ef","cmd-50c65f59883a42fe","cmd-2bd769aea3cf41c6","cmd-f3febff9ede44563","cmd-48baa941f97044f1","cmd-f746bb9939874ecc"]},{"id":"req-4","requirement":"Configuration is durable/persistent (not just runtime-only ip/nmcli state)","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1's interface addresses and forwarding sysctls were written to /etc/network/interfaces.d/vlan-routing.conf and /etc/sysctl.d/99-antrieb.conf; node2/node3 addressing and routes were persisted via nmcli connection profiles. This was independently confirmed by a real reboot on all three nodes after which addresses, forwarding, routes, and cross-VLAN ping all recovered without manual reapplication.","evidence_ids":["cmd-64e5dc8578c543a5","cmd-fb7834152e404440","cmd-f5f02fd1f61f480f","cmd-b10411b5dc5d49d1","cmd-d83a7682b16b4858","cmd-1d72f43c095941a8"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"Chronological review of state-changing executor commands shows each mutation maps to a material outcome or a necessary prerequisite: cmd-cb28dfaa410e47ee's flush of docker0/interfaces-config/networking.service/dhclient on node1 was needed to stop SONiC's interface reconciler from reverting the manual eth1/eth2 assignment, and the subsequent IP/route/sysctl commands on all three nodes directly implement the routing requirement. The three native reboots (cmd-2099c31ca14b40f5, cmd-80702735044e4a35, cmd-49dba734dd6f4008) are the independent restart-evidence protocol, executed sequentially with clear pre/post boot_id capture and recovery verification; the many failed/timeout boot_id polls immediately surrounding each reboot are expected transient connection loss during reboot, not unnecessary mutations. No troubleshooting experiments or unrelated mutations were found. Minor residue: the reboot helper left /tmp/reboot.log on node2 and node3, a small, low-risk, unremoved artifact rather than a reverted one.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cb28dfaa410e47ee","cmd-2099c31ca14b40f5","cmd-80702735044e4a35","cmd-49dba734dd6f4008","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a correct routed topology on node1 (two subnetted, forwarding-enabled interfaces) and demonstrated genuine cross-VLAN reachability between node2 and node3 via ttl-consistent pings, both before and after persisting the configuration through real reboots of all three nodes. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was completed for every managed node with clear boot-id transitions and full state recovery. Hygiene is good; the only blemish is an unremoved /tmp/reboot.log left by the reboot helper on node2/node3, which is bounded and low-risk.","limitations":["Lifecycle after_prepare is an alias of before_prepare (prepare disabled), so all observed changes are attributable to the executor by construction; this is expected given the task configuration and not evidence of any gap.","Boot-id polling produced several transient connection-reset/timeout entries during each reboot window; these are expected artifacts of the node being offline mid-reboot and were correctly not treated as failures."]},"ttft_ms":18723,"type":"result","duration_ms":57352,"uuid":"32d95bfc-99f3-478a-9284-31dec585f75f","ttft_stream_ms":6386,"time_to_request_ms":36,"first_content_frame_ms":6935,"queued_turn_count":0}
